    'g-faq-q1':       'Q. Is there an absolute right answer in pairing?',
    'g-faq-a1':       "No. Pairing principles are guidelines based on taste science and cultural experience — not absolute rules. Individual palates differ, and the same person may feel differently depending on mood, health, and context. Learn the principles, but let your own pleasure be the final judge.",
    'g-faq-q2':       'Q. What if I can\'t find a good anju for soju?',
    'g-faq-a2':       "Soju's greatest strength is its neutral flavour — it doesn't clash with anything. Whatever is in your fridge — kimchi, a rolled omelette, tofu — makes a fine soju anju. Anything salty or oily will work.",
    'g-faq-q3':       'Q. Where should a wine beginner start?',
    'g-faq-a3':       "The most reliable entry point is cheese and wine. Soft cheese like brie or Camembert with a light white (Chardonnay, Pinot Grigio); or cheddar/Gouda with a medium-bodied red (Merlot, Shiraz). Cheese is a forgiving partner that works with almost every wine style.",
    'g-faq-q4':       'Q. Why do I crave makgeolli and pajeon on a rainy day?',
    'g-faq-a4':       "Multiple factors work together. First, the sizzle of pancake batter shares a similar frequency with falling rain — a genuine auditory association. Second, cold, damp weather triggers a biological pull toward warm, comforting, oily food. Third, this pairing has been repeated so many times in Korean culture that it has become a deeply conditioned cultural reflex.",
    'g-faq-q5':       'Q. Where should a whisky beginner start?',
